== Motivation ==
The ability to create an unspendable script that commits to arbitrary data is
already possible using <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> and other mechanisms. However, there
is no standardized descriptor to specifically represent these unspendable
scripts, making implementations prone to errors and security risks. Introducing
the <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor provides a clear and consistent method for
hardware wallets and other tools to handle and interpret these scripts,
promoting better interoperability and reducing ambiguity.

== Specification ==

=== Unspendable Script Without Data ===
If no data is specified, the <tt>unspendable()</tt> descriptor produces an
<tt>OP_RETURN</tt> script with no additional data:

<pre>
unspendable()
</pre>

This results in the following script:

<pre>
OP_RETURN
</pre>

This ensures a provably unspendable output without committing to any specific
data.

=== Unspendable Script With Data ===
If arbitrary data is provided, the <tt>unspendable(DATA)</tt> descriptor
produces an <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> script followed by the specified data:

<pre>
unspendable(DATA)
</pre>

Where <tt>DATA</tt> is a hex-encoded byte sequence. This results in the
following script:

<pre>
OP_RETURN <DATA>
</pre>

=== Semantics ===
The <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor represents a complete script expression that
consists solely of an <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> opcode. If <tt>DATA</tt> is provided,
it follows the <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> opcode, ensuring both unspendability and a
verifiable commitment to the data. If no <tt>DATA</tt> is provided, the script
consists of a bare <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> opcode. It must constitute the entire
script or, when used in Taproot, an independent script leaf. This results in a
provably unspendable script, as per Bitcoin's protocol rules.

Example:

For an <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor with <tt>DATA</tt> set to the ASCII
string "Hello, Bitcoin!" (hex-encoded as
<tt>48656c6c6f2c20426974636f696e21</tt>), the descriptor and resulting script
are:

* Descriptor:
<pre>
unspendable(48656c6c6f2c20426974636f696e21)
</pre>

* Resulting Script:
<pre>
OP_RETURN 48656c6c6f2c20426974636f696e21
</pre>

=== Integration with Taproot's <tt>tr()</tt> Descriptor ===
The <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor can also be included as a stand-alone
Taproot script leaf, ensuring that it remains isolated as an independent script
and does not interact with other script logic. Additionally, it can coexist with
other spendable script leaves within a Taproot Merkle tree.

==== Descriptor Syntax ====
An example of a Taproot descriptor with a single script leaf that is
unspendable:

<pre>
tr(KEY, unspendable(DATA))
</pre>

Where:

* <tt>KEY</tt> is the internal key used for key-path spending.
* <tt>unspendable(DATA)</tt> is an unspendable script leaf in the Taproot Merkle tree.

In this construction, the default spending path remains via the internal key
(<tt>KEY</tt>). However, the Taproot script tree contains an explicitly
unspendable script.

An example of a Taproot descriptor with multiple script leaves, only one of
which is unspendable:

<pre>
tr(KEY, {pk(KEY2), unspendable(DATA)})
</pre>

Where:

* <tt>KEY</tt> is the internal key used for key-path spending.
* <tt>pk(KEY2)</tt> is a script leaf that allows spending with <tt>KEY2</tt>.
* <tt>unspendable(DATA)</tt> is an unspendable script leaf in the Taproot Merkle tree.

In this construction, the default spending path remains via the internal key
(<tt>KEY</tt>). However, the Taproot script tree contains multiple script
leaves, including an explicitly unspendable spend path. This ensures that even
if the script path is revealed, it does not introduce unintended spending
conditions. Other script leaves, such as <tt>pk(KEY2)</tt>, remain fully
functional and spendable.

== Rationale ==
The <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor abstracts the technical details of creating
unspendable <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> scripts, providing a clear and user-friendly
method to define scripts that both prevent spending and serve as a commitment to
arbitrary data. This standardization facilitates consistent handling across
various Bitcoin applications and enhances the clarity of intent when embedding
data in the blockchain.

There are many scenarios where embedding arbitrary data in the blockchain,
independent of spending conditions, is valuable. For instance, in Client-Side
Verification protocols, users rely on on-chain commitments to verify the
integrity of off-chain data. The script opcodes that enable these use cases
already exist; the <tt>unspendable(DATA)</tt> descriptor simply standardizes and
formalizes a practice that is already in use. By doing so, it guarantees a
consistent, secure, and reliable method for embedding data in cryptographic
proofs.

=== Why Existing Methods Are Insufficient ===
Existing approaches to defining unspendable outputs, such as using the
<tt>raw(SCRIPT)</tt> descriptor, are suboptimal. The <tt>raw</tt> descriptor
does not enforce spendability constraints, requiring hardware wallets and other
signing tools to manually inspect the script for an <tt>OP_RETURN</tt> opcode.
This adds complexity and increases the risk of misclassification.

In contrast, the <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor explicitly indicates that a
script cannot be spent, simplifying transaction auditing and reducing developer
burden. It is also simpler and less risky for hardware wallets to support, since
it is guaranteed to be unspendable—a clear advantage over the broadly flexible
<tt>raw</tt> descriptor.

Moreover, relying on the <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor discourages developers
from crafting custom unspendable scripts, which are prone to errors. A malformed
script could inadvertently resemble a valid spendable script, potentially
allowing trivial spending. By design, <tt>unspendable</tt> ensures the script
remains provably unspendable, regardless of any committed data.

== Compatibility ==
This proposal is fully compatible with existing Bitcoin protocols and does not
introduce any changes to transaction validation rules. It solely provides a
standardized descriptor for constructing unspendable scripts using existing
script capabilities.

== Reference Implementation ==
A reference implementation of the <tt>unspendable</tt> descriptor will be
provided as a pull request to the
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin Bitcoin Core] repository, demonstrating its
integration and usage within the wallet and transaction creation processes.

